Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\langle -4,-1,-3\rangle$ and $b=\langle 3,0,2\rangle$. Place $a$ above $b$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
a\times b&=\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\-4&-1&-3\\3&0&2\end{vmatrix}\\&=\begin{vmatrix}-1&-3\\0&2\end{vmatrix}i-\begin{vmatrix}-4&-3\\3&2\end{vmatrix}j+\begin{vmatrix}-4&-1\\3&0\end{vmatrix}k\\&=((-1)(2)-(-3)(0))i\\&\quad-((-4)(2)-(-3)(3))j\\&\quad+((-4)(0)-(-1)(3))k\\&=((-2)-(0))i\\&\quad-((-8)-(-9))j\\&\quad+((0)-(-3))k\\&=-2i-j+3k\\&=\langle -2,-1,3\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\langle 2,-2,-2\rangle$ and $b=\langle 1,0,-2\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
b\times a&=\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\1&0&-2\\2&-2&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&=\begin{vmatrix}0&-2\\-2&-2\end{vmatrix}i-\begin{vmatrix}1&-2\\2&-2\end{vmatrix}j+\begin{vmatrix}1&0\\2&-2\end{vmatrix}k\\&=((0)(-2)-(-2)(-2))i\\&\quad-((1)(-2)-(-2)(2))j\\&\quad+((1)(-2)-(0)(2))k\\&=((0)-(4))i\\&\quad-((-2)-(-4))j\\&\quad+((-2)-(0))k\\&=-4i-2j-2k\\&=\langle -4,-2,-2\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\langle 0,1,-4\rangle$ and $b=\langle -1,3,-1\rangle$. Place $a$ above $b$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
a\times b&=\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\0&1&-4\\-1&3&-1\end{vmatrix}\\&=\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\3&-1\end{vmatrix}i-\begin{vmatrix}0&-4\\-1&-1\end{vmatrix}j+\begin{vmatrix}0&1\\-1&3\end{vmatrix}k\\&=((1)(-1)-(-4)(3))i\\&\quad-((0)(-1)-(-4)(-1))j\\&\quad+((0)(3)-(1)(-1))k\\&=((-1)-(-12))i\\&\quad-((0)-(4))j\\&\quad+((0)-(-1))k\\&=11i+4j+k\\&=\langle 11,4,1\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\langle 3,2,-3\rangle$ and $b=\langle 0,0,3\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
b\times a&=\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\0&0&3\\3&2&-3\end{vmatrix}\\&=\begin{vmatrix}0&3\\2&-3\end{vmatrix}i-\begin{vmatrix}0&3\\3&-3\end{vmatrix}j+\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\3&2\end{vmatrix}k\\&=((0)(-3)-(3)(2))i\\&\quad-((0)(-3)-(3)(3))j\\&\quad+((0)(2)-(0)(3))k\\&=((0)-(6))i\\&\quad-((0)-(9))j\\&\quad+((0)-(0))k\\&=-6i+9j\\&=\langle -6,9,0\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

Read the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\langle 3,4,4\rangle$ and $b=\langle 0,4,0\rangle$. Place $b$ above $a$ and expand along the top row with signs $+,-,+$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
b\times a&=\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\0&4&0\\3&4&4\end{vmatrix}\\&=\begin{vmatrix}4&0\\4&4\end{vmatrix}i-\begin{vmatrix}0&0\\3&4\end{vmatrix}j+\begin{vmatrix}0&4\\3&4\end{vmatrix}k\\&=((4)(4)-(0)(4))i\\&\quad-((0)(4)-(0)(3))j\\&\quad+((0)(4)-(4)(3))k\\&=((16)-(0))i\\&\quad-((0)-(0))j\\&\quad+((0)-(12))k\\&=16i-12k\\&=\langle 16,0,-12\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. In each partial derivative, hold the other variable constant.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2y+y^3)-\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2+xy)\\
&=2xy-x.
\end{aligned}
$$

The term $y^3$ is constant with respect to $x$, and $x^2$ is constant with respect to $y$.

For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}
&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\bigl(x\ln(x+y)\bigr)\\
&=\ln(x+y)+\frac{x}{x+y},\\[4pt]
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
&=\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\bigl(y e^{xy}\bigr)\\
&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_x-P_y&=\ln(x+y)+\frac{x}{x+y}-e^{xy}-xy e^{xy}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The point $(1,1)$ lies in the domain because $1+1>0$. Evaluating gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
(Q_x-P_y)(1,1)&=\ln 2+\frac12-e-e\\
&=\ln 2+\frac12-2e.
\end{aligned}
$$

For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. Differentiate before substituting the point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\sin(xy)-\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2+y^2)\\
&=y\cos(xy)-2y.
\end{aligned}
$$

The factor $y$ in the first term comes from the chain rule. At $(1,\pi)$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
(Q_x-P_y)(1,\pi)&=\pi\cos(\pi)-2\pi\\
&=-\pi-2\pi\\
&=-3\pi.
\end{aligned}
$$

For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.

$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_x&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(3 x y + 2 y^{2}\right)\\
&=3 y\\
P_y&=\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(- x^{2} - 3 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\right)\\
&=- \frac{3}{x + y}\\
Q_x-P_y&=\left(3 y\right)-\left(- \frac{3}{x + y}\right)\\
&=3 y + \frac{3}{x + y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate first, then substitute the point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(Q_x-P_y)(0,\frac{1}{2})&=\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)-\left(-6\right)\\
&=\frac{15}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

